.TH TUI_MENU_INIT 3 2026-11-09 "libtuikit 0.8" "libtuikit Manual"
.SH NAME
tui_menu_init, tui_menu_set, tui_menu_select, tui_menu_selected,
tui_menu_count, tui_menu_handle, tui_menu_render, tui_menu_format \-
the main menu of a program with several main views
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <tuikit/menu.h>
.PP
.BI "void tui_menu_init(tui_menu *" m );
.BI "int  tui_menu_set(tui_menu *" m ", const tui_menu_entry *" entries ", int " n ", int " selected );
.BI "void tui_menu_select(tui_menu *" m ", int " i );
.BI "int  tui_menu_selected(const tui_menu *" m );
.BI "int  tui_menu_count(const tui_menu *" m );
.BI "tui_menu_result tui_menu_handle(tui_menu *" m ", const tui_event *" ev );
.BI "void tui_menu_render(const tui_menu *" m ", tui_canvas " c );
.BI "int  tui_menu_format(const tui_menu *" m ", int " rows ", int " cols ", char *" out ", size_t " outsz );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tui_menu
is the main menu: one entry per main view, each with a number as hot key
(1 to 9), a live count and a description.
An entry is
.IR "{ label, count, attention, description }" :
.I count
below 0 shows no count,
.I attention
above 0 adds the number of counted items needing attention.
.PP
.BR tui_menu_render ()
draws on the canvas it is given (the free area between the top bars and the
status line).
The rows are left-aligned in a block that is centered, a little above the
middle so that the block and the description below it look centred together;
there is no title.
A row reads
.B "> 1  Label  N (M)"
\(en the cursor row has the
.B >
mark and the role
.BR selection ;
the hot key is in the text colour;
.B (M)
is in the warning colour (bold orange, not yet a scheme role);
no words are written in the row.
The description of the entry under the cursor is wrapped to two or three
centered lines below the block (at most 60 columns, the last line cut with an
ellipsis).
At least three blank rows separate the last entry from the description.
The block is sized for the longest description (three lines), so the entries
keep their rows for every cursor position.
On a short canvas the gap shrinks to what fits, never below one row, and a
description that does not fit is cut before any entry.
A resize needs nothing but the next render.
.PP
.BR tui_menu_set ()
sets the entries (at most
.B TUI_MENU_MAX
(9); the array and its strings are not copied, so a program may change the
counts in it and draw again) and the cursor, which is clamped; it fails with
.B EINVAL
for more entries, a NULL array or a NULL label.
.BR tui_menu_select ()
moves the cursor (clamped);
.BR tui_menu_selected ()
and
.BR tui_menu_count ()
read it back.
.PP
.BR tui_menu_handle ()
moves the cursor on Up and Down (and k and j) and on Home and End, without
wrapping, and returns
.B TUI_MENU_MOVED
when it moved.
Enter returns
.BR TUI_MENU_ACTIVATED ;
so does a digit 1 to 9 that names an entry, after moving the cursor to it.
Everything else is
.BR TUI_MENU_IGNORED .
.PP
.BR tui_menu_format ()
writes the layout as plain text for a canvas of
.I rows
by
.I cols
(one line per row, each ending in a newline, trailing blanks not written);
it returns the bytes written or \-1 when
.I outsz
was too small.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR libtuikit (7),
.BR tui_statusline_init (3),
.BR tui_scheme_load (3)
